  "rules": {
    "rk71_offer_and_claim_audit_v1": "The exact product page displays SKU B07VVVC143, a price of 49.99 dollars, and a 75-percent score across 12 reviews. It advertises Bluetooth and detachable-USB wired modes, connection to as many as five devices, an Fn-plus-Space five-second factory reset, a compact 71-key layout without a numpad, and red-switch and other color or switch variants. The same page first says RGB Backlight but later explicitly says the RK71 has no multicolored RGB and only single-LED backlighting with 17 dynamic modes. Report the narrower explicit description and flag the wording conflict. Price, aggregate score, and seller durability or comfort language are marketplace observations, not a long-term failure rate or an independent work-use test.",
    "rk71_same_model_use_reports_v1": "One same-model hobbyist says the RK71 is used as a cheap wireless projector keyboard and would not be recommended because Escape requires Fn, defaults to tilde, and cannot be reprogrammed; another participant suggests AutoHotkey as a workaround. A second same-model thread reports that software connection was tricky for one user and that a buyer two days into ownership could not connect the software even in wired mode and saw two device PIDs; another participant suggests contacting support or seeking replacement if the board is faulty, but the thread records no confirmed diagnosis or outcome. These pages establish concrete layout and software-detection risks to test. They do not establish population prevalence, months-later chatter, switch death, or warranty performance.",
    "chatter_diagnosis_boundary_v1": "The electrical-switch reference explains that contact bounce, also called chatter, is a general mechanical-contact phenomenon: springy contacts can bounce after striking and create rapidly pulsed current that fast logic may misread as a data stream. It also describes circuit- or firmware-level debouncing methods such as low-pass filtering, repeated sampling for a steady state, latches, or Schmitt triggers. That mechanism makes repeated electrical inputs physically plausible in general, but it does not identify the RK71's switches, measure this model, or diagnose an Escape-layer design or software-detection failure. Before invoking chatter, separate layout behavior, wireless versus wired connection, software recognition, whole-board failure, and repeatable key-specific duplicate events. The available material does not establish that a user can repair this model or replace its switches.",
    "rk71_writer_purchase_protocol_v1": "Before keeping an RK71 for full-time writing or coding, verify the exact variant, current seller, return deadline, and warranty rather than assuming them from the frozen page. Decide first whether a 71-key board without a numpad, Fn-dependent Escape behavior, and single-color rather than multicolored backlighting are acceptable. During the return window, test every key and shortcut in a key-event viewer, Escape and tilde in the actual editor and terminal, software recognition in wired mode, Bluetooth switching on every intended device, wake and reconnect behavior, and a sustained typing sample. Record the state before using the documented Fn-plus-Space reset, then repeat tests after reset. A duplicate-key symptom should be reproduced across applications, devices, and wired or wireless modes before treating contact bounce as a candidate; an unresolved software-detection, connection, or repeat-input problem should trigger support or return rather than an unsupported repair claim. Continue only if the board passes the buyer's work-critical checks and the residual durability uncertainty is acceptable.",
    "evidence_bounded_rk71_reliability_decision_v1": "The available evidence supports neither a declaration that the RK71 is proven reliable for years nor a declaration that it commonly chatters or dies after months. The seller page supplies an exact low-cost offer and aggregate score but no longitudinal failure denominator. The same-model threads expose an Escape-layer limitation and an unresolved two-day software-detection problem, not months-later duplicate inputs. The switch reference explains a possible general mechanism but cannot diagnose this model. Treat the RK71 as a conditional, returnable trial if its compact layout and single-color lighting fit the buyer and the board passes the predeclared work-use checks; otherwise defer or choose another board. Report any result as this unit's bounded outcome, not a model-wide failure rate."
